so we're once again in the middle of a  climate change summit and as is often  the case during these climate change  summits we are starting to see articles  uh outraged um about the fact that many  world leaders and others like jeff bezos  flew to in this case it's glasgow  scotland to the climate change summit on  private jets and the contrast that is  illuminated by these articles is of  course about we're trying to figure out  climate change and yet private jet on a  per person basis  outrageous carbon footprint a private  jet where you might have two pilots and  a flight attendant and two people or you  know maybe a small entourage  on a per-person basis an outrageous  uh expenditure of carbon emissions and  um it is something that happens every  time there is one of these climate  summits and it's an issue and i'm going  to talk about it but i actually believe  that it's part of the distraction from  the real issue and i'll explain to you  in a moment so here's just one sample  article  around 400 private jets  carrying heads of state billionaires  business leaders and celebrities have  converged on glasgow for a global  warming summit amazon founder jeff  bezos's 65 million us dollar gulf stream  jet was among the hundreds of vip jets  reportedly carrying more than a thousand  attendees and their staff to the cop26  meeting cop26 with others including  microsoft founder bill gates actor  arnold schwarzenegger also expected to  fly in  it can't be stressed enough how bad  private jets are for the environment it  is the worst way to travel by miles says  matt finch from the uk's transport and  environment campaign group the average  private jet and we are not talking air  force one emits two tons of co2 every  hour in flight private jets are very  prestigious but it is difficult to avoid  the hypocrisy of using one while  claiming to be fighting climate change  he said for context the total carbon  footprint of an ordinary citizen  including everywhere they travel and  everything they consume is around eight  tons a year so basically like just your  flight to cop26 is two tons  per hour per hour  and um most normal people um consume you  know about eight tons a year in carbon  emissions in total the 400 private  aircraft are expected to emit 13 000  tons of carbon dioxide but uk secretary  of state liz trust defended the number  of planes flying in saying it's  important that we have people face to  face i think everybody who's ever done a  zoom call knows they're quite useful for  some things but when you really get into  crunch negotiations when you want to  look somebody in the eye and talk to  them face to face you do need to meet in  person so  listen um i i hate to tell people  the solution is not going to be to ban  private jets  so let's take it piece by piece  does this happen at every climate change  summit yes do people get angry every  time yes is there something legitimately  hypocritical about saying we want to  deal with climate but i'm going to  expend  a family's worth of annual co2 emissions  just to fly myself in this case to  glasgow scotland yes that that's all  absolutely true and legitimate but  there's two different things that are  going on here first is just a kind of  like a pragmatism  um the  economics of private jets for these  elites for lack of a better term  um dignitaries billionaires politicians  etc the economics of the private jet  makes sense and it's been calculated uh  ad nauseam if you're a ceo and you've  got to go to the airport uh typically  the airports you fly out of commercially  are far more congested and in traffic  laden areas and you've got to get there  an hour and a half before the flight or  maybe longer depending on what's going  on um and then you've got to do security  boarding all these different things then  get out of the airport just the  additional time  on the basis of of what ceos get paid  makes it worth it to do the private jet  now you could say well they shouldn't be  getting paid that much okay these are  all debates we can have but i want to  first start from just like a completely  pragmatic thing the private jets aren't  going away for these people private jets  aren't going to be banned i don't even  know what the  legal framework would be to ban private  jets like this just this stuff just  isn't going to happen we might want to  do some of these things it's not going  to happen so the solution here is not  going to be to ban private jets all  right that's number one in terms of like  the technological question here the  solution on private jet emissions is  going to be renewable fuels  solar-powered planes prototypes already  exist at some point it's going to be a  real thing we have to be realistic as  far as that goes i am 110 on board  in saying look at the per person carbon  emissions for getting these people to  glasgow it's obscene it's crazy it's  particularly counterproductive when  we're talking about dealing with climate  fine but it's not going to change  now  um  the more important thing  is  for me the private jet thing  is  almost as big of a distraction as saying  you should recycle  your orange juice  containers  and the boxes your amazon stuff comes in  and stop using plastic straws as a look  away from the real problem issue which  is 100 companies are responsible for 71  of global emissions and you not using  plastic straws is not really going to  change that reminder  the guardian's been on this for four  years  100 companies are responsible for 71 of  global emissions a relatively small  number of fossil fuel producers and  their investors could hold the key to  tackling climate change just 100  companies have been the source of more  than 70 percent of the world's  greenhouse gas emissions since 1988 the  carbon majors report pinpoints how a  relatively small set of fossil fuel  producers may hold the key to systemic  change on carbon emissions we've known  this for a very long time  even commercial air travel  accounts for only three to four percent  of total  u.s greenhouse gas emissions so  here we're talking about private jets  private jets are a smaller emitter of  carbon emissions than the commercial  airline industry commercial airliners  are responsible for three to four  percent of u.s greenhouse gas emissions  the private jets are a fraction of that  so the finger pointing at the jets and  the air travel  for me  is almost the same distraction as saying  you're not did you just throw out  that paper bag instead of recycling it  or reusing it the biggest polluters are  thrilled to have articles about plastic  straws  they're thrilled to have articles about  people are trashing half of what they  could be recycling and they're almost as  thrilled to have articles about look at  the private jets being sent to glasgow  are the optics bad of course  of course they're bad but the private  jets aren't going away they can  eventually be shifted to renewable  sources of energy we already have the  prototypes it's going to be a while and  it is another one of these distractions  from the fact that unregulated or barely  regulated or not enough regulated major  industry has not been told by  governments  you're not going to be able to keep  polluting in this way 100 companies 71  of global emissions so i'm not laughing  off the private jet thing but it's  important to contextualize it lastly  i also don't care that much about the  private jets  if really substantive change comes from  the talks which it rarely does  often these talks are we all agree very  seriously very strongly that climate  change is a problem  let's set  vague  maybe achievable completely  unenforceable goals  and end up putting barely a dent in the  greenhouse gas emissions that we're  pumping into the atmosphere so  i really wouldn't care about the private  jets if we had something enforceable and  more serious come out of these talks the  the cycle is likely to continue  it's